  <p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Months 2 and 3</strong> carry the rest of the one-time work &mdash; the schema graph, the FAQ content, the thin pages, the architecture and the four geo landing pages &mdash; alongside the same two content pieces, four Google Posts, report and call every month.</p>

  <div class="sec-head" style="margin-top:52px"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2>What changes from month four</h2></div>
  <div class="esc">
    <span class="tag">Stated now, not when you ask</span>
    <h3>The one-time fixes run out. The output goes up instead of the price coming down.</h3>
    <p>By the end of month three the technical and structural work is finished &mdash; it does not need doing twice. Rather than charge the same for less, the monthly quota expands. Same $2,250, more production.</p>
    <div class="escgrid">
      <div>
        <h5>Content</h5>
        <b>2 pieces <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span> 3 pieces a month</b>
        <p>Nine of the roughly fifteen clusters identified in the gap analysis are still unbuilt at the end of month three. This is what clears them.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h5>Geo coverage</h5>
        <b>4 pages <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span> ongoing expansion</b>
        <p>The first four cover Dallas, Fort Worth and the two highest-volume service terms. Next comes Plano, Frisco, Southlake and Flower Mound &mdash; where the competitors in the gap set actually sit.</p>
